1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,766
You just saved a trending AI workflow into your favorites

2
00:00:04,033 --> 00:00:07,066
And scheduled tomorrow down to every half hour

3
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:11,066
Before bed you even bought a course that promises to teach you a side hustle

4
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:13,966
It looks like you worked much harder than yesterday

5
00:00:14,133 --> 00:00:15,066
But a week later

6
00:00:15,066 --> 00:00:16,900
The job is still the same job

7
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:18,533
The money is still the same money

8
00:00:18,833 --> 00:00:22,700
And the things you want to do are still lying in a folder called start later

9
00:00:23,033 --> 00:00:24,733
Most people aren't not working hard

10
00:00:24,833 --> 00:00:29,466
They're doing something at every step yet not one thing changes their position

11
00:00:29,933 --> 00:00:31,866
That sounds a bit abstract

12
00:00:32,066 --> 00:00:34,966
But you've definitely seen what it looks like in real life

13
00:00:35,266 --> 00:00:36,433
Someone wants to start a side hustle

14
00:00:36,433 --> 00:00:37,633
So they learn video editing first

15
00:00:37,633 --> 00:00:39,266
Then research account names

16
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:42,266
Then spend two days picking an avatar and cover fonts

17
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:44,266
And finally write seven versions of a bio

18
00:00:44,266 --> 00:00:50,033
But ask them to post the first piece of content or ask a stranger if they'd pay and they start saying

19
00:00:50,133 --> 00:00:51,700
I'm not ready yet

20
00:00:52,066 --> 00:00:53,266
Someone wants to change jobs

21
00:00:53,266 --> 00:00:55,133
They scroll through tons of career advice

22
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:56,866
Take a few personality tests

23
00:00:57,033 --> 00:00:58,933
Save a pile of resume templates

24
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:03,233
But the portfolio that really needs reworking never gets opened

25
00:01:03,633 --> 00:01:05,366
Someone else wants to get healthier

26
00:01:05,433 --> 00:01:07,133
They buy a pair of running shoes first

27
00:01:07,466 --> 00:01:09,100
Download three tracking apps

28
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:10,466
Watch ten workout videos

29
00:01:10,866 --> 00:01:13,966
But that running track downstairs they don't visit it once all week

30
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:15,700
These things aren't useless

31
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:18,700
The problem is they make it too easy to fool yourself

32
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:22,066
After finishing them you feel like you're already moving forward

33
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:24,966
But what really decides whether you leave where you are

34
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,166
Isn't how many actions you completed today

35
00:01:27,266 --> 00:01:32,533
It's whether those actions put you in a position where results come easier

36
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,433
This episode won't teach you to build a perfect life plan

37
00:01:36,466 --> 00:01:40,466
Or push you to quit your job and go all in or force yourself into a corner

38
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:43,100
We're only talking about one simpler judgment

39
00:01:43,333 --> 00:01:46,366
Tactics solve how to take the step right in front of you

40
00:01:46,633 --> 00:01:49,766
Strategy decides where you stand after taking it

41
00:01:50,033 --> 00:01:53,900
When tools templates and advice are everywhere within arm's reach

42
00:01:54,100 --> 00:01:56,366
The scarcest thing is no longer the next move

43
00:01:56,466 --> 00:01:59,300
It's knowing which step is worth taking at all

44
00:02:01,033 --> 00:02:02,300
It's eleven thirty at night

45
00:02:02,300 --> 00:02:04,533
You only meant to look at your phone for five minutes

46
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:06,133
Then you scroll onto a video

47
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:10,366
Titled three AI workflows every ordinary person must learn

48
00:02:11,033 --> 00:02:12,733
You watch it and it makes total sense

49
00:02:12,733 --> 00:02:13,866
You save it with one tap

50
00:02:13,866 --> 00:02:16,133
Then you see someone else talking about side hustles

51
00:02:16,433 --> 00:02:19,933
They say if you're making content now you have to build an automation system first

52
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:21,333
You click in again

53
00:02:21,533 --> 00:02:22,300
Then further down

54
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:24,433
Someone shows off their Notion template

55
00:02:24,533 --> 00:02:26,300
Someone shares a set of prompts

56
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:29,500
Someone says skip this tool and you'll fall behind fast

57
00:02:29,933 --> 00:02:31,300
The more you watch the more anxious you get

58
00:02:31,633 --> 00:02:33,533
And the more you feel you can't stop

59
00:02:33,733 --> 00:02:35,300
So you start collecting

60
00:02:35,333 --> 00:02:36,466
Saving links

61
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:37,633
Organizing folders

62
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:39,333
Tagging your materials

63
00:02:39,400 --> 00:02:41,766
You even seriously write up a study plan

64
00:02:42,033 --> 00:02:43,000
When you turn off the light

65
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:44,200
You feel a little satisfied

66
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:45,666
Today's scrolling wasn't wasted

67
00:02:45,933 --> 00:02:47,033
But the next day at work

68
00:02:47,033 --> 00:02:49,133
Your boss hands you a real problem

69
00:02:49,633 --> 00:02:53,033
A client asks you what exactly can this thing solve for me

70
00:02:53,066 --> 00:02:54,766
And suddenly you still can't answer

71
00:02:55,233 --> 00:02:56,500
The tools are still there

72
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:58,100
The methods weren't wrong either

73
00:02:58,233 --> 00:03:00,866
But they never automatically became your judgment

74
00:03:01,100 --> 00:03:02,433
What keeps people spinning in place the most

75
00:03:02,433 --> 00:03:03,500
Usually isn't laziness

76
00:03:03,633 --> 00:03:06,066
It's the busyness that looks so much like progress

77
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:11,033
Because the actions that actually change your position are usually not that comfortable

78
00:03:11,233 --> 00:03:12,700
They expose you

79
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:15,233
You have to put what you made out into the world

80
00:03:15,233 --> 00:03:16,933
You have to say the price out loud

81
00:03:17,133 --> 00:03:19,033
You have to hear people say no thanks

82
00:03:19,033 --> 00:03:23,066
You have to admit the plan you polished for so long might be something nobody wants

83
00:03:23,266 --> 00:03:24,700
Compared to these moments

84
00:03:24,700 --> 00:03:26,966
Researching a new tool feels so much safer

85
00:03:27,266 --> 00:03:29,466
Tweaking your profile page one more time feels so much cozier

86
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:32,133
Watching other people's advice forever feels so much more dignified

87
00:03:32,233 --> 00:03:38,966
So many people quietly pour their energy into tasks they can finish instantly but reality never tests

88
00:03:39,266 --> 00:03:41,033
Making a beautiful planning spreadsheet

89
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:43,233
Formatting documents until everything is neat

90
00:03:43,633 --> 00:03:45,433
Learning ten new keyboard shortcuts

91
00:03:45,533 --> 00:03:48,566
These actions give you a tiny sense of control

92
00:03:48,700 --> 00:03:52,033
Like hammering a nail into a blank life

93
00:03:52,133 --> 00:03:55,733
But sometimes they just organize your anxiety more neatly

94
00:03:55,866 --> 00:03:57,733
Without moving the problem forward one inch

95
00:03:57,800 --> 00:04:00,266
Think about that person preparing a side hustle

96
00:04:00,300 --> 00:04:01,500
They learned editing

97
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:02,533
They can make posters

98
00:04:02,533 --> 00:04:04,600
They even registered a really nice-sounding name

99
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:07,200
But if they've never asked a single person

100
00:04:07,200 --> 00:04:09,233
Would you actually pay for this

101
00:04:09,233 --> 00:04:11,566
Then what they're practicing probably isn't entrepreneurship

102
00:04:11,666 --> 00:04:14,766
It's turning entrepreneurship into a very polished hobby room

103
00:04:15,033 --> 00:04:16,533
This isn't mocking them

104
00:04:16,633 --> 00:04:18,233
A lot of people are like this

105
00:04:18,333 --> 00:04:20,366
Because the market's answer is too blunt

106
00:04:20,533 --> 00:04:22,933
A friend not liking your post might just mean they didn't see it

107
00:04:23,033 --> 00:04:26,666
But a real customer refusing to pay gives you a reason you can't dodge

108
00:04:26,933 --> 00:04:29,166
The same thing happens with learning

109
00:04:29,300 --> 00:04:30,333
You finish a course

110
00:04:30,333 --> 00:04:32,466
And feel the teacher explained it so clearly

111
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:35,900
But when a colleague asks so how do we actually change things tomorrow

112
00:04:36,066 --> 00:04:38,166
All you have left are a few pretty words

113
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:39,066
Models

114
00:04:39,066 --> 00:04:39,800
Frameworks

115
00:04:39,800 --> 00:04:40,833
Long-termism

116
00:04:40,866 --> 00:04:41,900
None of them are wrong

117
00:04:42,233 --> 00:04:44,966
They just haven't reached the place where you can actually use them

118
00:04:45,233 --> 00:04:47,833
Today's AI tools amplify this illusion

119
00:04:47,933 --> 00:04:49,933
It hands you a full plan in minutes

120
00:04:49,933 --> 00:04:51,100
It makes posters for you

121
00:04:51,100 --> 00:04:51,866
Writes copy

122
00:04:51,866 --> 00:04:52,766
Lists steps

123
00:04:52,933 --> 00:04:56,266
Even generates a roadmap that looks completely finished

124
00:04:56,600 --> 00:04:59,233
Starting something really is easier than before

125
00:04:59,333 --> 00:05:01,733
But easy to start doesn't mean easy to arrive

126
00:05:01,866 --> 00:05:04,566
When everyone can get a first draft quickly

127
00:05:04,633 --> 00:05:07,466
What actually separates people becomes something else

128
00:05:07,900 --> 00:05:10,066
Do you know who actually has the problem

129
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:13,266
Are you willing to put your work out there for feedback

130
00:05:13,433 --> 00:05:17,133
Can you cut the part you cherish most because the feedback says so

131
00:05:17,400 --> 00:05:19,300
Tools can speed up your actions

132
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:22,233
But they can't decide which actions deserve speeding up

133
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:25,433
That's why some people spin like tops all day

134
00:05:25,466 --> 00:05:26,733
Yet feel emptier at night

135
00:05:27,233 --> 00:05:28,833
It's not that they finished nothing

136
00:05:28,833 --> 00:05:32,533
It's that nothing they finished made tomorrow hold one more possibility

137
00:05:32,933 --> 00:05:34,200
You think you're accumulating

138
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:37,233
But you're really just refilling your to-do list

139
00:05:37,433 --> 00:05:39,933
Real progress doesn't necessarily feel good right away

140
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:43,066
It usually shows you an ugly answer first

141
00:05:43,266 --> 00:05:44,833
My work isn't good enough yet

142
00:05:44,833 --> 00:05:47,466
My customers aren't who I thought they were

143
00:05:47,666 --> 00:05:51,566
What I want to do needs fundamentals first not tools

144
00:05:51,733 --> 00:05:53,500
None of these answers are easy

145
00:05:53,700 --> 00:05:55,866
But they push you to the next square

146
00:05:55,933 --> 00:06:00,333
Instead of standing in the same spot polishing the same floor tile brighter

147
00:06:01,833 --> 00:06:03,166
Picture a chessboard

148
00:06:03,200 --> 00:06:07,233
Early in the game players often make moves that seem meaningless

149
00:06:07,700 --> 00:06:09,700
A piece nudged a little toward the center

150
00:06:09,700 --> 00:06:11,366
A line given up for now

151
00:06:11,400 --> 00:06:13,966
A defense that doesn't score anything immediately

152
00:06:14,100 --> 00:06:15,333
Spectators watching will think

153
00:06:15,333 --> 00:06:16,433
What's the point of that

154
00:06:16,466 --> 00:06:18,166
You didn't capture a single piece

155
00:06:18,433 --> 00:06:19,400
But the player knows

156
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:21,766
The squares on the board were never equal

157
00:06:21,866 --> 00:06:24,566
Some squares open up more directions for you

158
00:06:24,600 --> 00:06:27,066
Some squares block your own paths

159
00:06:27,466 --> 00:06:29,033
Some squares look safe

160
00:06:29,033 --> 00:06:32,233
But the moment you stand there you can only react to others

161
00:06:32,333 --> 00:06:34,100
Life isn't a chess match of course

162
00:06:34,100 --> 00:06:36,066
There's no need to treat other people as opponents

163
00:06:36,266 --> 00:06:39,033
And you don't have to make anyone worse off just to win

164
00:06:39,266 --> 00:06:41,666
But the board offers one useful reminder

165
00:06:41,733 --> 00:06:45,300
Where you stand each day decides how many paths you can walk tomorrow

166
00:06:45,633 --> 00:06:48,366
Someone held up entirely by next month's paycheck

167
00:06:48,433 --> 00:06:51,766
Has very little room to say no to a job they hate

168
00:06:52,033 --> 00:06:54,066
Someone with a little buffer in hand

169
00:06:54,066 --> 00:06:56,033
Even just one extra month of margin

170
00:06:56,033 --> 00:06:58,666
Can search for the next job with a calm head

171
00:06:58,833 --> 00:07:02,033
This doesn't mean everyone should immediately save up a huge pile of money

172
00:07:02,133 --> 00:07:03,466
It means you need to see

173
00:07:03,466 --> 00:07:05,633
That a buffer is itself a position

174
00:07:05,900 --> 00:07:06,733
Another example

175
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:08,766
Two designers with similar skills

176
00:07:08,833 --> 00:07:12,433
One turns every finished project into a case study

177
00:07:12,533 --> 00:07:15,333
And slowly people learn what problems they've solved

178
00:07:15,866 --> 00:07:17,633
The other does plenty of work

179
00:07:17,833 --> 00:07:21,433
But everything is scattered across chat logs and a desktop

180
00:07:21,466 --> 00:07:22,266
A year later

181
00:07:22,266 --> 00:07:24,166
You can't really say either one worked harder

182
00:07:24,200 --> 00:07:26,466
But the first one earns trust more easily

183
00:07:26,500 --> 00:07:28,333
They didn't magically gain more ability

184
00:07:28,333 --> 00:07:32,566
They placed the same ability where others could see it and judge it

185
00:07:32,900 --> 00:07:35,300
Position is sometimes a cash buffer

186
00:07:35,533 --> 00:07:38,266
Sometimes a body of work that can be seen

187
00:07:38,600 --> 00:07:41,900
Sometimes three people willing to give you honest feedback

188
00:07:42,100 --> 00:07:45,566
Sometimes one sentence you can say clearly inside a messy problem

189
00:07:45,900 --> 00:07:47,633
They all share one thing

190
00:07:47,700 --> 00:07:49,700
Not making you look impressive

191
00:07:49,733 --> 00:07:52,633
But making sure your next time doesn't start from zero

192
00:07:52,866 --> 00:07:56,100
You'll notice that truly useful effort always has a bit of compounding

193
00:07:56,233 --> 00:07:57,066
Done today

194
00:07:57,066 --> 00:07:58,333
Still usable tomorrow

195
00:07:58,400 --> 00:07:59,766
Others can find it too

196
00:07:59,933 --> 00:08:01,966
New options appear because of it

197
00:08:02,333 --> 00:08:04,700
But a lot of draining effort doesn't work that way

198
00:08:04,733 --> 00:08:07,500
Today's overtime only fills today's hole

199
00:08:07,666 --> 00:08:10,933
Today's anxiety only pushes you to save ten more videos

200
00:08:11,300 --> 00:08:14,266
Today's plan only buys you a moment of relief

201
00:08:14,500 --> 00:08:15,433
Tomorrow you wake up

202
00:08:15,433 --> 00:08:18,100
And have to prove you're not falling behind all over again

203
00:08:18,500 --> 00:08:21,700
So strategy isn't a sheet filled with five-year goals

204
00:08:21,933 --> 00:08:24,500
It's more like pausing now and then to ask

205
00:08:24,733 --> 00:08:26,433
Where exactly am I standing right now

206
00:08:26,433 --> 00:08:28,033
What am I most short of

207
00:08:28,033 --> 00:08:29,833
What do I already have in hand

208
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:33,266
Will this step in front of me add a path or close one

209
00:08:33,333 --> 00:08:36,466
These four questions matter more than finding ten trending methods

210
00:08:36,633 --> 00:08:38,466
Because methods expire fast

211
00:08:38,633 --> 00:08:40,133
A software updates

212
00:08:40,133 --> 00:08:41,900
A platform changes its rules

213
00:08:41,900 --> 00:08:46,300
A content format that seems to be working can lose its audience next month

214
00:08:46,800 --> 00:08:51,333
But your ability to spot problems make things collaborate and understand feedback

215
00:08:51,333 --> 00:08:53,533
Moves with you to the next place

216
00:08:53,866 --> 00:08:56,766
This is also what's easiest to overlook right now

217
00:08:57,266 --> 00:08:58,566
We're too quick to ask

218
00:08:58,600 --> 00:09:00,033
Which tool is the strongest

219
00:09:00,033 --> 00:09:01,833
Which set of prompts works best

220
00:09:01,833 --> 00:09:03,466
Which niche is still worth entering

221
00:09:03,533 --> 00:09:05,233
These questions are fine to ask

222
00:09:05,233 --> 00:09:07,133
But they should come later

223
00:09:07,300 --> 00:09:08,866
The question before them is

224
00:09:09,066 --> 00:09:14,066
Does this tool in my hand bring me closer to a real person and a real problem

225
00:09:14,633 --> 00:09:15,400
If it doesn't

226
00:09:15,400 --> 00:09:19,366
However new it is it might just be another toy that keeps you from feeling left behind

227
00:09:19,666 --> 00:09:20,466
If it does

228
00:09:20,466 --> 00:09:21,900
Then it's your lever

229
00:09:22,333 --> 00:09:24,700
A lever isn't something that lifts you up high

230
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:28,566
A lever is a fulcrum that lets the same effort move a bigger result

231
00:09:28,833 --> 00:09:30,900
Write an article nobody finishes reading

232
00:09:31,033 --> 00:09:33,100
And you've maybe just completed one article

233
00:09:33,233 --> 00:09:36,066
Write up a question clients keep asking again and again

234
00:09:36,133 --> 00:09:38,433
And make it findable by the next client

235
00:09:38,700 --> 00:09:40,866
Then it's more than one piece of writing

236
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,266
It starts holding a place for you

237
00:09:43,300 --> 00:09:44,333
Do one project

238
00:09:44,333 --> 00:09:45,633
Finish it and it's over

239
00:09:45,633 --> 00:09:47,066
That's one delivery

240
00:09:47,133 --> 00:09:50,433
Turn it into a method a case study and a reusable process

241
00:09:50,466 --> 00:09:53,100
And next time it no longer starts from zero

242
00:09:53,400 --> 00:09:56,700
This isn't telling you to package every little thing as a personal brand

243
00:09:56,800 --> 00:09:57,866
It's a reminder

244
00:09:57,866 --> 00:10:00,933
Don't pour all your strength into places where it vanishes after one use

245
00:10:01,100 --> 00:10:03,733
Some actions only leave you exhausted when they're done

246
00:10:03,833 --> 00:10:06,933
Other actions leave you a map when they're done

247
00:10:07,266 --> 00:10:09,733
That's the difference between tactics and strategy

248
00:10:09,866 --> 00:10:12,633
Tactics is how to get one thing done today

249
00:10:12,900 --> 00:10:17,100
Strategy is whether tomorrow walks easier after today's work is done

250
00:10:18,500 --> 00:10:19,633
Then Monday morning arrives

251
00:10:19,633 --> 00:10:21,500
You open your plan for the new week

252
00:10:21,600 --> 00:10:22,666
You want to sleep earlier

253
00:10:22,700 --> 00:10:23,633
Want to exercise

254
00:10:23,633 --> 00:10:24,500
Want to read

255
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:25,366
Want to learn AI

256
00:10:25,700 --> 00:10:27,066
Want to get the side hustle going

257
00:10:27,066 --> 00:10:28,566
And also want more time with family

258
00:10:28,700 --> 00:10:30,266
Every one of these is right

259
00:10:30,466 --> 00:10:32,966
So you give each one a slice of time

260
00:10:33,466 --> 00:10:35,033
Twenty minutes of exercise in the morning

261
00:10:35,033 --> 00:10:36,300
Audiobooks on the commute

262
00:10:36,433 --> 00:10:37,733
Tool tutorials at lunch

263
00:10:37,733 --> 00:10:39,000
Writing content at night

264
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:40,100
A review before bed

265
00:10:40,333 --> 00:10:41,766
It looks well rounded

266
00:10:42,033 --> 00:10:44,833
And it's easy to be gasping for air by Wednesday

267
00:10:45,133 --> 00:10:47,233
It's not that you lack time management skills

268
00:10:47,300 --> 00:10:50,866
It's that you spread limited energy evenly across too many directions

269
00:10:51,100 --> 00:10:53,766
So every direction ends up with nothing but a beginning

270
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:58,300
There's a kind of effort that's like giving one drop of water to ten dying plants

271
00:10:58,466 --> 00:10:59,633
You've been busy forever

272
00:10:59,633 --> 00:11:01,500
Yet not a single plant actually recovers

273
00:11:01,733 --> 00:11:04,466
Real focus isn't cutting everything out

274
00:11:04,666 --> 00:11:07,233
It's loosening the one spot that blocks you the most

275
00:11:07,500 --> 00:11:09,833
Take a very real scenario first

276
00:11:09,900 --> 00:11:12,700
Someone says every day they have no time for a side hustle

277
00:11:12,800 --> 00:11:15,733
But after work they're too exhausted to do anything but lie down

278
00:11:15,933 --> 00:11:19,366
If what's truly stuck is the gap in income and energy

279
00:11:19,433 --> 00:11:23,833
Then they don't need to learn editing marketing sales and writing all at once

280
00:11:24,066 --> 00:11:26,966
Nor do they need to cut two more hours of sleep

281
00:11:27,333 --> 00:11:29,300
What they might need to do first is

282
00:11:29,300 --> 00:11:32,366
Make the most repetitive task at work take less effort

283
00:11:32,600 --> 00:11:35,433
Get clear on one expense that's been blurry forever

284
00:11:35,600 --> 00:11:39,933
Or first test whether a small service they can reliably deliver is something anyone needs

285
00:11:40,133 --> 00:11:42,466
Not because these things sound the coolest

286
00:11:42,533 --> 00:11:44,300
But because once they loosen

287
00:11:44,300 --> 00:11:47,266
They hand back some time some energy or some certainty

288
00:11:47,633 --> 00:11:48,700
With that little bit of margin

289
00:11:48,700 --> 00:11:51,333
The choices after it stop depending on pure grit

290
00:11:51,333 --> 00:11:52,900
That's what finding the bottleneck means

291
00:11:53,033 --> 00:11:54,833
Not asking what do I most want to do

292
00:11:54,833 --> 00:11:59,533
But asking which problem drags every other effort back until it moves

293
00:12:00,066 --> 00:12:02,700
Someone thinks their bottleneck is not knowing how to use AI

294
00:12:02,933 --> 00:12:06,300
Actually their problem is not knowing what's bugging their customers

295
00:12:06,533 --> 00:12:08,700
Someone thinks they can't make content

296
00:12:08,700 --> 00:12:12,233
Actually their problem is fearing others will think they sound not good enough

297
00:12:12,500 --> 00:12:14,566
Someone thinks they lack discipline

298
00:12:14,733 --> 00:12:18,533
Actually they stay up late every night because their daytime tasks have no boundaries

299
00:12:18,600 --> 00:12:19,233
You see

300
00:12:19,233 --> 00:12:21,533
The surface of a problem is usually loud

301
00:12:21,666 --> 00:12:24,833
While the real block can hide somewhere very quiet

302
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:28,333
You don't have to diagnose every problem in your life at once

303
00:12:28,466 --> 00:12:30,866
Just pick one that keeps coming back

304
00:12:31,200 --> 00:12:34,466
The one whose appearance scatters all your other plans every time

305
00:12:34,600 --> 00:12:35,400
For example

306
00:12:35,466 --> 00:12:37,000
Every time you want to start making content

307
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,166
You spend two hours looking for references first

308
00:12:39,400 --> 00:12:41,666
Then feel worse and worse about yourself as you scroll

309
00:12:41,700 --> 00:12:43,466
And end up writing nothing at all

310
00:12:43,833 --> 00:12:46,100
Then what's blocking you may not be an inability to write

311
00:12:46,266 --> 00:12:49,366
It's not having an output mechanism that allows a rough start

312
00:12:49,666 --> 00:12:52,466
At this point studying ten more excellent accounts

313
00:12:52,533 --> 00:12:54,100
Only raises the bar higher

314
00:12:54,466 --> 00:12:58,633
The square you need to move to might be setting yourself one tiny rule

315
00:12:58,933 --> 00:13:02,133
Before looking at any reference write one hundred words of your own judgment

316
00:13:02,133 --> 00:13:03,666
Not to write well

317
00:13:03,900 --> 00:13:07,333
But to turn references from replacing your thinking into calibrating it

318
00:13:07,533 --> 00:13:08,366
Another example

319
00:13:08,466 --> 00:13:09,733
You've been wanting to change jobs

320
00:13:09,733 --> 00:13:12,233
But every time you open a job app you get more anxious

321
00:13:12,400 --> 00:13:15,166
You think what you need is a hundred more job listings

322
00:13:15,400 --> 00:13:19,633
But what you're really missing might be a portfolio that helps people grasp you fast

323
00:13:19,933 --> 00:13:21,733
Then the most valuable thing this week

324
00:13:21,733 --> 00:13:23,300
Isn't scrolling more postings

325
00:13:23,333 --> 00:13:26,633
It's writing up one concrete thing you've done clearly

326
00:13:26,833 --> 00:13:28,066
What problem you solved

327
00:13:28,066 --> 00:13:29,066
How you did it

328
00:13:29,066 --> 00:13:30,533
And what changed in the end

329
00:13:30,733 --> 00:13:32,266
Once that page is written

330
00:13:32,266 --> 00:13:33,666
Your position has changed

331
00:13:33,666 --> 00:13:36,466
You're no longer just someone anxiously waiting for a chance

332
00:13:36,733 --> 00:13:39,133
You now have something you can hand to people

333
00:13:39,400 --> 00:13:40,966
The bottleneck loosens a little

334
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:42,933
And every other action finally has somewhere to land

335
00:13:43,333 --> 00:13:44,900
Many people are afraid of focus

336
00:13:44,900 --> 00:13:48,766
Because they understand it as betting their whole life on one thing

337
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:49,933
It isn't

338
00:13:50,100 --> 00:13:52,633
Focus is more like for a stretch of time

339
00:13:52,833 --> 00:13:55,833
Admitting that the thing most worth saving first isn't everything

340
00:13:56,066 --> 00:13:58,033
You're not giving up your other wishes

341
00:13:58,033 --> 00:14:01,266
You're just choosing not to spend all of today on every single one

342
00:14:01,666 --> 00:14:04,533
Solving one problem that keeps dragging you down each month

343
00:14:04,833 --> 00:14:07,966
Might be slower than changing ten habits every day

344
00:14:08,066 --> 00:14:10,366
Yet it's far more likely to leave real change behind

345
00:14:10,400 --> 00:14:13,066
Don't rush to ask which niche has the best future

346
00:14:13,066 --> 00:14:13,733
Ask first

347
00:14:13,733 --> 00:14:16,566
Which kind of passivity should I get rid of right now

348
00:14:16,833 --> 00:14:19,533
Don't rush to ask which trick will turn my life around

349
00:14:19,533 --> 00:14:20,300
Ask first

350
00:14:20,300 --> 00:14:23,966
Which small action will make next week's me feel less pushed around

351
00:14:24,266 --> 00:14:27,166
Strategy isn't finding a path that's forever right

352
00:14:27,433 --> 00:14:33,100
Strategy is not letting every path get blocked on you at the same spot

353
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:34,766
At this point

354
00:14:34,766 --> 00:14:35,700
You might ask

355
00:14:35,900 --> 00:14:37,533
So what exactly should I do

356
00:14:37,733 --> 00:14:40,333
Do I need to write a five-year plan right now

357
00:14:40,333 --> 00:14:42,333
Break every quarter into goals

358
00:14:42,466 --> 00:14:44,566
And list dozens of key results

359
00:14:44,733 --> 00:14:46,966
If you love planning of course you can write one

360
00:14:47,033 --> 00:14:49,533
Just don't mistake that sheet for strategy itself

361
00:14:49,700 --> 00:14:50,900
However beautiful a sheet is

362
00:14:50,900 --> 00:14:53,466
It won't build a relationship with reality for you

363
00:14:53,633 --> 00:14:56,266
A genuinely useful start can be much smaller

364
00:14:56,466 --> 00:14:58,066
You only need one sheet of paper

365
00:14:58,100 --> 00:14:59,100
And three questions written down

366
00:14:59,666 --> 00:15:00,533
First question

367
00:15:00,700 --> 00:15:02,900
If nothing changes in six months

368
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:05,300
What's the one thing I least want to keep enduring

369
00:15:05,433 --> 00:15:07,166
Not some huge wish

370
00:15:07,200 --> 00:15:09,133
Not I want to become successful

371
00:15:09,300 --> 00:15:11,233
But one specific way you're trapped

372
00:15:11,233 --> 00:15:12,000
For example

373
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:15,433
I don't want to feel tense three days before every payday

374
00:15:15,800 --> 00:15:20,166
I don't want a shelf of projects but not one case that shows my worth

375
00:15:20,600 --> 00:15:24,733
I don't want to panic about being obsolete every time a new tool appears

376
00:15:25,133 --> 00:15:27,733
Write the days you don't want to keep living clearly first

377
00:15:27,833 --> 00:15:29,100
Not to scare yourself

378
00:15:29,266 --> 00:15:32,900
But to know which direction deserves to move first

379
00:15:33,433 --> 00:15:34,300
Second question

380
00:15:34,533 --> 00:15:38,100
What do I already have in hand that could be rearranged

381
00:15:38,233 --> 00:15:40,100
Maybe the projects you've done

382
00:15:40,100 --> 00:15:42,900
Maybe two evenings a week you can free up

383
00:15:43,033 --> 00:15:45,733
Maybe a friend willing to tell you the truth

384
00:15:46,033 --> 00:15:49,066
Maybe a type of problem you already know inside out from work

385
00:15:49,533 --> 00:15:53,966
Or maybe just the patience to finish one small thing for a whole month

386
00:15:54,300 --> 00:15:56,533
Don't stare at what you don't have yet

387
00:15:56,733 --> 00:15:58,966
Strategy isn't pretending you have plenty of resources

388
00:15:59,033 --> 00:16:02,033
Strategy is seeing the few cards in your hand clearly first

389
00:16:02,400 --> 00:16:03,366
Third question

390
00:16:03,633 --> 00:16:05,266
Is there one action this week

391
00:16:05,266 --> 00:16:08,300
That once done makes reality give me a new answer

392
00:16:08,866 --> 00:16:10,300
The key word is reality

393
00:16:10,433 --> 00:16:12,333
Not another feeling you give yourself

394
00:16:12,600 --> 00:16:14,733
But an action that gets a reply back

395
00:16:15,066 --> 00:16:16,300
You want to build a course

396
00:16:16,300 --> 00:16:17,533
Don't record ten lessons first

397
00:16:17,633 --> 00:16:21,633
Talk to three people about what they most want solved first

398
00:16:21,700 --> 00:16:22,833
You want to do design

399
00:16:22,833 --> 00:16:24,933
Don't keep your work hidden on a hard drive

400
00:16:25,133 --> 00:16:28,966
Send your most finished piece to one person who might actually use it

401
00:16:29,200 --> 00:16:30,400
You want to switch jobs

402
00:16:30,400 --> 00:16:32,266
Don't scroll postings until you're numb

403
00:16:32,300 --> 00:16:34,933
Write one project's results up as a single page

404
00:16:35,266 --> 00:16:36,200
You want to learn AI

405
00:16:36,200 --> 00:16:38,100
Don't install every tool first

406
00:16:38,100 --> 00:16:42,866
Take one real task you're doing today and see if it can save you half an hour

407
00:16:43,066 --> 00:16:45,633
None of these actions guarantee instant success

408
00:16:45,700 --> 00:16:47,766
But they get you information

409
00:16:48,100 --> 00:16:49,133
What people want

410
00:16:49,133 --> 00:16:50,300
What nobody gets

411
00:16:50,433 --> 00:16:51,533
What needs filling in

412
00:16:51,533 --> 00:16:53,266
What isn't worth continuing at all

413
00:16:53,633 --> 00:16:54,466
With information

414
00:16:54,466 --> 00:16:55,900
You've earned the right to adjust

415
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,500
Many people treat adjusting as giving up

416
00:16:58,666 --> 00:16:59,933
The morning routine failed

417
00:16:59,933 --> 00:17:01,766
So they decide they have no willpower

418
00:17:01,900 --> 00:17:02,933
Content numbers are bad

419
00:17:02,933 --> 00:17:04,733
So they decide they're not cut out for it

420
00:17:04,866 --> 00:17:06,300
A tool didn't stick

421
00:17:06,300 --> 00:17:08,133
So they decide they've fallen behind again

422
00:17:08,233 --> 00:17:11,933
But real strategy was never about guarding the first method you thought of

423
00:17:12,666 --> 00:17:14,033
The direction can stay steady

424
00:17:14,033 --> 00:17:15,433
The methods have to change

425
00:17:15,600 --> 00:17:17,833
Wanting a healthier body

426
00:17:17,833 --> 00:17:20,866
Doesn't mean you must run at six a m forever

427
00:17:21,066 --> 00:17:23,033
Wanting more options in your career

428
00:17:23,033 --> 00:17:25,500
Doesn't mean only one side hustle format can work

429
00:17:25,733 --> 00:17:26,833
Wanting better efficiency

430
00:17:26,833 --> 00:17:29,100
Doesn't mean learning every new tool either

431
00:17:29,500 --> 00:17:30,833
Set aside ten minutes a week

432
00:17:30,833 --> 00:17:32,266
To review only three things

433
00:17:32,433 --> 00:17:35,266
Which actions this week actually brought me closer to reality

434
00:17:35,333 --> 00:17:38,233
Which actions only soothed me for not falling behind

435
00:17:38,500 --> 00:17:41,466
Which one will I cut next week and which will I keep

436
00:17:41,700 --> 00:17:43,633
Reviewing isn't grading yourself

437
00:17:43,800 --> 00:17:45,833
Not stamping finished as good guy

438
00:17:46,066 --> 00:17:47,933
And unfinished as bad guy

439
00:17:48,133 --> 00:17:50,433
A review just helps you switch paths

440
00:17:50,833 --> 00:17:54,366
So you stop banging your head against the same wall in the same blocked spot

441
00:17:54,533 --> 00:17:56,033
Life isn't a chess match

442
00:17:56,200 --> 00:17:58,833
You don't need to trap anyone to move forward

443
00:17:59,033 --> 00:18:02,266
And you don't need to burn your own bridges to prove you're brave

444
00:18:02,533 --> 00:18:03,533
What really matters

445
00:18:03,533 --> 00:18:06,900
Is not staying stuck for long in a position that keeps getting narrower

446
00:18:07,100 --> 00:18:09,466
Next time you see a trending method

447
00:18:09,500 --> 00:18:10,933
Don't save it right away

448
00:18:11,200 --> 00:18:13,933
And don't rush to wonder if you've fallen behind

449
00:18:14,066 --> 00:18:15,100
Ask one question first

450
00:18:15,100 --> 00:18:17,866
Which real problem in front of me can it help me handle

451
00:18:18,233 --> 00:18:19,200
If you can't answer

452
00:18:19,200 --> 00:18:22,100
It might just be another thing that calms you down for a moment

453
00:18:22,433 --> 00:18:23,600
If you can answer

454
00:18:23,600 --> 00:18:25,900
Then stop waiting for a more perfect version

455
00:18:26,033 --> 00:18:28,766
Use it to take an action that gets a reply

456
00:18:29,400 --> 00:18:30,200
Post it

457
00:18:30,200 --> 00:18:31,033
Ask it

458
00:18:31,033 --> 00:18:31,933
Hand it over

459
00:18:32,100 --> 00:18:34,533
You don't need to see your whole life clearly at once

460
00:18:34,833 --> 00:18:36,300
You just need today's step

461
00:18:36,300 --> 00:18:38,233
To open one more path than yesterday

462
00:18:38,466 --> 00:18:40,300
Tactics can keep you busy

463
00:18:40,500 --> 00:18:43,966
Only strategy makes sure you don't stay busy in the same spot forever
